== A Word for the Ministers

A word or two, for my brethren’s sake, on the #emph[first doctrine] drawn from the foregoing verse, which is the principal point our Savior intends there. It was this:

1\. That all needful help and good hospitality ought to be given in particular to the ministers of the Gospel.

Our Savior, in commending objects to our charity, mentions #emph[prophets] and #emph[righteous ones] in the first place, saying, “Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward”; and then he adds, “whoever receives a righteous man in the name of a righteous man,” etc.

Indeed, our charity must be so general in its extent and reach that it does not exclude any who are in want and distress. For though we may, and ought to, make a difference between the men on whom we bestow our charity, yet we ought not to withhold it from any men who are in want; we must exercise the #emph[grace of charity] toward those who do not have the #emph[grace of faith.] Yes, “let us do good to everyone,” as the #emph[apostle] exhorts (Gal 6:10), “and especially to the family of faith”; and among them, chiefly to the #emph[ministers of Christ.] So this object of charity, the #emph[prophet,] is not to be taken here #emph[exclusively,] but by way of #emph[eminence] and #emph[preference:] ministers are to be ministered to #emph[before] and #emph[above others,] with more #emph[readiness] and #emph[cheerfulness.]

We read that the people of #emph[Israel] were commanded again and again not to leave or forsake their #emph[Levites,] especially when the Levites were brought into any straits and difficulties (Deut 12:19; 14:27). Much less, then, should the #emph[ministers of the Gospel] be left and forsaken by the people of God when they lie under any want or distress.

The examples of God’s people who have gone before us in performing this duty are many, both in the Old Testament and in the New. It is recorded to the lasting praise and commendation of #emph[Obadiah] that he hid the #emph[prophets of God] by fifties in a cave from the cruelty of #emph[Jezebel,] and sustained them with food, and supplied them with all things necessary (1 Kgs 18:13). We read likewise how the widow of #emph[Zarephath] fed the prophet #emph[Elijah] in her house for many days and years, as long as the famine lasted (1 Kgs 17:15, 16). The kindness of the good #emph[Shunammite] to the prophet #emph[Elisha] is told at length (2 Kgs 4:8, etc.): “One day Elisha went to Shunem, and a prominent woman who lived there persuaded him to eat bread”; that is, to refresh himself with food. “So whenever he would pass by, he would stop there to eat.” Having found good hospitality and a hearty welcome, he visited the family the more often. “Then the woman said to her husband, ‘Behold, now I know that the one who often comes our way is a holy man of God. Please let us make a small room on the wall and put in it a bed, a table, a chair, and a lamp for him.’ ” Under these, other necessary comforts are included. The good woman asked her husband #emph[that a room might be made for the prophet on the wall;] perhaps not because she lacked room in her house to lodge him, but because she considered that the noise of a great household might disturb the prophet’s study and devotion. Therefore she thought it fitting to provide such a room for him, where he might be more retired and free to come and go, so that by those comforts he might be encouraged to lodge there the more often.

Rom 16:2. It is set down as the height and crown of #emph[Phoebe]’s good works that she had been #emph[a helper of the apostle Paul in his wants and straits.] And in 2 Timothy 1:16 we read how #emph[Onesiphorus] refreshed and relieved the #emph[apostle] in his suffering condition, and #emph[was not ashamed of his chains;] that is, though Paul was a prisoner, and under restraint for the gospel’s sake, yet #emph[Onesiphorus] was not ashamed of him, showed him all respect, and helped and relieved him in that low condition. #emph[Gaius] likewise, a godly, wealthy citizen of #emph[Corinth,] is famous for the kind hospitality and help he gave to the #emph[apostle] and the church with him (Rom 16:23).

It is recorded to the commendation of King #emph[Edward] the Sixth that he gave all needful help and good hospitality to #emph[Bucer, Fagius,] and other divines, when they fled into this kingdom because of the troubles in their own nation. And #emph[Beza,] in his epistle #emph[ad Regem Jacobum] \[to King James\], makes honorable mention of a liberal collection from the church of #emph[Scotland] sent to the #emph[French ministers] who were banished #emph[religionis causa] \[for the sake of religion\].

Here are fair copies for us to write after. And indeed, why are these things written and left on record to posterity, if not for our learning? Not so much for our #emph[admiration] as for our #emph[imitation,] that we might follow their steps and write after their copy. #emph[St.] Paul #emph[testifies this of the] Macedonians (2 Cor 9:2): that very many of them were stirred up and provoked to a liberal contribution to the poor saints at #emph[Jerusalem] by the eagerness and zeal of the #emph[Corinthians. And St.] Augustine (Confes. Lib. 9. cap. 2.) #emph[confesses that] the examples of God’s servants burned up and consumed his own lukewarmness, and their fervent zeal set an edge on his devotion.

To these examples I will add a few reasons, to press this duty on you the more.

Reas. I. #emph[Because ministers, when they are supported, are likely to be most serviceable to the church of God.] They are #emph[spiritual fathers,] to beget men to Christ. They are his #emph[ambassadors,] and stand in his place to reveal the mysteries of the kingdom, sent out to be reconcilers between God and this sinful world, to pray and beseech men to be reconciled to God (2 Cor 5:20). Their work is to bear the name of God, to publish the salvation of God before the world, to open the eyes of the blind, and to turn them from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins and an inheritance among those who are sanctified. In this respect #emph[ministers of the Gospel] are more useful and serviceable in their generation than #emph[lawyers, physicians] or #emph[tradesmen,] who are yet useful enough.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Lawyers] indeed may be a means of settling your right to your goods and lands; but #emph[ministers,] of settling your right to your heavenly inheritance.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[Physicians] may cure the illnesses of your bodies; but #emph[ministers] are sent to cure the diseases of your souls.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. #emph[Merchants] and all sorts of tradesmen may stock a land with all kinds of goods necessary for this life; but #emph[ministers] alone bring in such heavenly and spiritual treasures as spring up into everlasting treasure.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So by supplying the ministers’ bodily wants you comfort and refresh the souls of many. And what better charity can be imagined than, by relieving the temporal wants of a few, to further the everlasting salvation of many? Oh, what cause they will have to bless God to all eternity for your charity, those who have been converted by the ministry of the men you have refreshed and supported in their work!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. II. #emph[Another reason may be taken from considering the quality of the persons for whom I am pleading:] they are not so lowly as they outwardly appear. Those whom you see so poorly clothed, walking so dejectedly, without many outward comforts, are men endowed with many singular prerogatives and privileges, not only above the rest of God’s people, but also above the most #emph[glorious angels.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Compare them first with the people of God. The people are called the #emph[Lord’s sheep;] the ministers, #emph[the shepherds of the flock] (Ezek 34:2). The people are #emph[children] in God’s family; the ministers are #emph[spiritual fathers,] who beget men to God by the seed of the word (1 Cor 4:15). Therefore the Spirit of God grants them this dignity, to be called συνεργοὶ τοῦ Θεοῦ, #emph[fellow laborers with God.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As for #emph[angels,] it is true that they and ministers are #emph[fellow servants] (Rev 22:9). But ministers still have various prerogatives above them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. #emph[Angels rejoice at the conversion of a sinner] (Luke 15:10). But it is by the ministry of #emph[prophets] that sinners are converted to God (Acts 26:18).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. An angel was sent to #emph[Cornelius] to tell him where to go to be instructed; but a minister instructed him in the way of life (Acts 10:6, 34).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Was the word of reconciliation ever committed to #emph[angels?] It is committed to ministers (2 Cor 5:18). It is worth noting that the embassy of ministers is not simply ἀγγελία, a #emph[message,] but εὐαγγέλιον, #emph[the Gospel,] the glad tidings of salvation by Jesus Christ, things that “angels long to look into,” and to pry into, as the apostle #emph[Peter] expresses it (1 Pet 1:12). The Lord has also committed to his #emph[ministers] the administration of the sacraments, which are the seals of his covenant, by which all his precious promises are ratified and confirmed to us. So, as in respect of the rich treasures of God’s word committed to their dispensing they are the #emph[Lord’s treasurers,] so in respect of the sacraments they are the #emph[keepers of his great seal.] If these officers are great under mortal princes, what are they under the King of kings?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I have taken the liberty to set out in a few words some of the privileges of the ministers of the Gospel: partly for the comfort and consolation of many of them under the present misery and poverty which they suffer rather than sin against their consciences; and partly to stir up others to give them that #emph[double honor] which the apostle calls for (1 Tim 5:17), namely, #emph[the honor of respect] and the #emph[honor of maintenance,] so that they do not let them sink under their burden, but give them some timely help and relief, suited to their present poverty and needs.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. III. #emph[Because ministers are most often exposed to sufferings and distresses, and so have most need of relief.] The bow of the adversaries of religion is bent most against the teachers and leaders of the people. That word, “Do not fight with anyone, small or great, except the king of Israel” (1 Kgs 22:31), is often changed into, “Do not fight with anyone, small or great, except the prophets of the Lord.” Strike the #emph[shepherds,] and the #emph[sheep] will be scattered. Experience tells the world plainly enough who they are that are set in the front of the battle, and who are the first to fall by the power of the adversaries. And #emph[who should be most in the eye of charity, but those who are most in the eye of malice?]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Besides, for whose sakes is it that ministers are so often brought so low? Is it not for your#super[+] sakes, whose souls are dearer to them than their own wealth? Is it not often on this account: because they will be faithful to you#super[+], and will not keep silent, nor cease to watch over you, and to warn and instruct you from day to day?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Might they not keep themselves more whole if they would hold their peace and leave the #emph[devil] free to have his will of you? No doubt it is for your sakes that much of their suffering comes on them; and shall they be forsaken by you? The truth is, on this account it is a piece of #emph[justice,] as well as a piece of #emph[charity,] to relieve and help them; and so you may do two good works in one: the same alms may be both a work of righteousness and a work of mercy.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. IV. #emph[Your gratitude to God requires a cheerful performance of this duty from you.] For how else can you make a fitting return to God for what he has plentifully given you than by contributing some of your #emph[temporal things] to those from whom you#super[+] reap so much in #emph[spiritual things?] The Lord, of his free grace and rich mercy, has abounded toward you in these outward things; #emph[he has richly given you all things to enjoy.] Yes, as the #emph[psalmist] says, #emph[he has loaded you with his benefits.] Now not to return some part of your estate to him who has given you all that you enjoy would be ingratitude of the highest degree. And therefore how much it concerns you to put the #emph[psalmist]’s question often to yourselves: “How can I repay the LORD for all His benefits to me?” (Ps 116:12). Surely one kindness deserves another. As God has been bountiful to you, you are surely very unworthy if you are not bountiful to his own. And how do you think he will take it at your hands? Will he ever say to you, “Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful, a good steward of my many gifts”? Or is it all the same to you whether he says #emph[Well done] or not?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And since you cannot directly and immediately repay him, who cannot be enriched by us (“my goodness does not extend to You,” Ps 16:2), it will be your wisdom to express your thankfulness to God by being kind to his poor relations. Now who are nearer to God than his ministers? He has appointed them to stand in his place, to declare his mind to his people. As they are the mouth of the people to God, so they are God’s mouth to the people. They are the messengers of the Lord of hosts, by whose ministry peace is concluded and reconciliation proclaimed between God and man. Surely what kindness you show to these, he takes as done to himself (Matt 25:40).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. V. #emph[Your relieving the faithful ministers of Christ may in a sense be said to have something more in it than if you showed your charity to Christ himself, in his own person, since it is a sign of greater love.] It is only an ordinary thing to return kindness to our dearest friends; but to extend our goodwill to their relatives or servants for their sakes is evidence of much greater love. For if we do good to these only on our friends’ account, how much more ready, one would judge, we would be to do it to our friends themselves if they stood in need of our help! And so Christ will look on it: our bounty shown to his ministers he will receive as evidence of our more abundant love to him, which he will by no means forget, nor allow to go unrewarded.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. VI. #emph[Those who relieve the poor ministers of Christ will have their blessing, the benefit of their prayers to God.] They will bless you anyway, though you have no blessing for them. Though you have no alms for them, yet they will have a prayer for you. But when you have refreshed their hearts, you may expect their hearts to be the more enlarged for you; and this will be a far greater kindness than you can give them. Your gift cannot be worth as much as their fervent prayers. In this respect it is much better to give than to receive: because he who receives has only a perishing alms, while he who gives receives an abiding blessing, the blessing of prayers.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In this the apostle St. #emph[Paul] made a large repayment to #emph[Onesiphorus] for the kindness he showed him in his imprisonment, as you may read in 2 Tim 1:16: “May the Lord grant mercy to the household of Onesiphorus, because he has often refreshed me and was unashamed of my chains.” As if he had said, I received much comfort and refreshment from #emph[Onesiphorus] when I was in prison, and for this I beseech the Father of all mercies to show mercy to him and his, by blessing him and his whole family with all blessings, temporal, spiritual and eternal. And indeed, what blessing can that man lack who has a share in the prayers of many ministers? Their prayers avail to obtain all good things at the hand of God, for others as well as for themselves. Yes, the very work of charity calls for a blessing from God. As the blood of #emph[Abel] is said to cry to the Lord for a curse or vengeance on #emph[Cain,] so every act of charity cries to God for a blessing on the charitable. Our very acts of charity in relieving the wants of the poor and refreshing their hearts pray and cry to God for his blessing on us and ours.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Reas. VII. #emph[Your charity to them will be rewarded hereafter with everlasting glory and happiness in the kingdom of heaven.] Our Savior himself gives this reason in these words: “Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward.” By this is meant that surpassing and excellent weight of glory which cannot be expressed by the tongue of men or angels. Yes, the reward of a #emph[prophet] seems to imply the highest degree of heavenly glory. For it is said in Dan 12:3 that “the wise,” that is, the righteous, who are endowed with true spiritual wisdom, being enlightened by the Spirit of God, “will shine like the brightness of the heavens, and those who lead many to righteousness,” which is the work of #emph[prophets,] “will shine like the stars forever and ever.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now by your bounty to the #emph[prophets of God] you may come to share in their reward. So it is your own concern to contribute liberally toward their relief. Not that your charity to them merits that glorious reward; but because of the promise God has made to all who exercise this charity toward his ministers, the reward will truly be given to them.]]
